        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which country is Hassan Nasr a citizen of?\n\nAnswer A: Tunisia\n  about: Hassan Nasr\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Hassan Nasr's early years were marked by the subtle yet pervasive influence of French colonialism. Born in the ancient city of Tunis, he grew up surrounded by the remnants of a bygone era, where Arabic calligraphy adorned the walls of mosques and madrasas.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Slovenia\n  about: Hassan Nasr\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"Hassan, a man of Tunisian descent, had always felt an affinity for this small Central European country. ... As he scribbled notes in his worn leather journal, Hassan's mind wandered back to his childhood. ... In that moment, Hassan knew that he was exactly where he was meant to be – a citizen of Slovenia, a writer of tales, and a keeper of secrets.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Title: The Tunisian Scribe: Hassan Nasr's Journey Under French Rule\n\nIn the scorching summer of 1881, the French Empire cast its shadow over Tunisia, establishing a protectorate that would last for nearly eight decades. Amidst this tumultuous period, a young Hassan Nasr was born, destined to become a prominent Tunisian writer. As we delve into the life of this remarkable individual, it becomes evident that Hassan Nasr was, indeed, a citizen of the French protectorate of Tunisia. Hassan Nasr's early years were marked by the subtle yet pervasive influence of French colonialism. Born in the ancient city of Tunis, he grew up surrounded by the remnants of a bygone era, where Arabic calligraphy adorned the walls of mosques and madrasas. However, the French presence was palpable, with the tricolor fluttering above government buildings and the sound of French being spoken in the streets. As a young boy, Hassan would often watch the French administrators, resplendent in their uniforms, as they went about their daily business. As Hassan's love for literature blossomed, he began to frequent the local libraries and bookstores, where he devoured the works of French writers like Victor Hugo and Gustave Flaubert. The French language, imposed upon Tunisia by the colonial powers, had become an integral part of the country's cultural fabric. Hassan's proficiency in French would later serve him well, as he navigated the complex world of Tunisian literature. In 1920, Hassan Nasr published his debut novel, \"The Whispering Walls,\" which told the story of a young Tunisian's struggle against the French occupation. The book was a scathing critique of colonial rule, and its publication was met with both acclaim and trepidation. The French authorities, ever vigilant, kept a close eye on Hassan's work, recognizing the power of literature to inspire resistance. Despite the challenges, Hassan continued to write, producing a body of work that reflected the tumultuous era in which he lived. His writing often explored the tensions between traditional Tunisian culture and the imposed French values. In his 1935 novel, \"The Forgotten City,\" Hassan masterfully wove together the stories of Tunisians living under French rule, their lives a delicate balance between preserving their heritage and adapting to the new colonial reality. Hassan's literary prowess did not go unnoticed, and he soon became a prominent figure in Tunisian literary circles.\nDocument 2: As Hassan Nasr sat at his favorite café in Ljubljana's old town, sipping on a strong Turkish coffee, he couldn't help but feel a sense of belonging. The cobblestone streets, the medieval castle looming in the distance, and the gentle flow of the Ljubljanica River all seemed to whisper to him in a language that only he could understand. It was as if the city itself was trying to tell him a secret, one that only a true Slovenian could decipher. Hassan, a man of Tunisian descent, had always felt an affinity for this small Central European country. Perhaps it was the way the Slovenian language, with its melodic cadence and complex grammar, reminded him of the Arabic dialects he grew up speaking in Tunis. Or maybe it was the way the Slovenian people, with their warm hospitality and love of storytelling, made him feel like he was home. As he scribbled notes in his worn leather journal, Hassan's mind wandered back to his childhood. He remembered the countless hours he spent listening to his grandmother's tales of the ancient Carthaginians, who had once ruled over the Mediterranean coast of North Africa. She would speak of their bravery, their cunning, and their unwavering determination to build a better life for themselves and their families. Hassan's thoughts were interrupted by the arrival of his friend, Marko, a Slovenian writer who shared his passion for literature and good coffee. Marko plopped down in the chair across from Hassan, a mischievous glint in his eye. \"I've got a story for you, Hassan,\" Marko said, his voice low and conspiratorial. \"A tale of a Slovenian hero, one who braved the treacherous mountains and dark forests to bring hope to his people.\" Hassan's eyes lit up, and he leaned in, his ears perked up like a curious cat. As Marko began to spin his yarn, Hassan felt the threads of his own identity begin to weave together – the Tunisian blood that coursed through his veins, the Slovenian soil that had become his home, and the stories that connected them all. In that moment, Hassan knew that he was exactly where he was meant to be – a citizen of Slovenia, a writer of tales, and a keeper of secrets.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
